<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  The Mac App Store version of Weft (scripts/mac-appstore.sh). The App Sandbox is required there; this is all the app asks of it:
  - network client: collaboration (WebRTC to the others, the public relays that introduce them). Not "server": the web view does the WebRTC
    sockets itself, which the app's own sandbox does not stand in the way of - tried in a sandboxed build (two peer connections in the page,
    host and STUN candidates, the data channel opens) with and without it, no difference; and Apple's review refused the app for having it
    ("does not appear to have matching functionality"). The local interface for AI assistants (control.rs), the only thing in Weft that
    listens, does not run in the sandbox.
  - files chosen in a dialog or handed over by the Finder, read and write, and the bookmarks that keep that access for the next start
    (src-tauri/src/sandbox.rs): "Zuletzt geöffnet", autosave, the shared folder (Nextcloud ...).
  The first two keys are the app's identity at Apple (the team of src-tauri/gen/apple/project.yml); a build for trying the sandbox on this
  Mac (`scripts/mac-appstore.sh test`) leaves them out - they only work together with the provisioning profile.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.application-identifier</key>
	<string>7GD6JHBABJ.eu.townwork.weft</string>
	<key>com.apple.developer.team-identifier</key>
	<string>7GD6JHBABJ</string>
	<key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-write</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.files.bookmarks.app-scope</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
